 ## Subtopics
 
-### Differences-based Compression (Mensmann)
-A hybrid compression scheme that combines variable-length coding, delta encoding, and brick assembly [S1]. It differs from the Smelyanskiy scheme by requiring explicit resolution of variable-length coding and delta encoding during decompression, rather than relying on pre-computed shuffle patterns for fixed-bit blocks [S1]. It is chosen when variable bit-depths are needed across a volume with complex spatial coherence, but the overhead of variable-length decoding is acceptable [S1]. It implements individual kernels for each supported bit length to handle the variable addressing modes required by variable-length coding [S1]. The delta encoding is implemented by adding the calculated delta value to the existing value in the volume, rather than overwriting it [S1].
+### [Differences-based Compression (Mensmann)](5733-differences-based-compression-mensmann.md)
+Differences-based Compression (Mensmann) is a hybrid compression scheme that resolves variable-length coding, delta encoding, and brick assembly during decompression. It sets itself apart from its siblings by implementing individual kernels for each supported bit length to handle the variable-length coding addressing modes, while performing delta encoding by adding calculated values to existing volume data rather than overwriting it.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Decompression is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, with a small constant factor for the shuffle lookup and SIMD expansion [S1][S2]. The overhead of lookup tables is negligible [S1][S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Storage is $N \times ibits$ bits per block, plus a small constant overhead for the base value and bit-depth indicator [S2]. The shift pattern table is negligible in size [S1][S2].
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Scales well with SIMD width (e.g., 128-bit SSE processes 8 elements per instruction) [S1][S2].
-- **Strengths:** Supports variable bit-depths per block, efficient SIMD expansion, low decompression overhead [S1][S2].
-- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of shuffle patterns, less flexible than fully variable-length schemes for highly heterogeneous data [S1][S2].
-- **When to use:** When variable bit-depths are beneficial and SIMD acceleration is available [S1][S2].
-- **When not to use:** When fixed-rate compression is sufficient or when variable-length decoding overhead is too high [S1].
-
+**Time Complexity:** Decompression is efficient due to SIMD parallelism. The overhead of lookup tables is negligible [S1][S2].
+**Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the shift pattern table, which is small and constant size relative to the number of supported bit depths [S1][S2].
+**Performance:** Optimized for 128-bit SSE, processing eight elements per instruction [S1][S2].
+**Strengths:** Efficient handling of variable bit-depths via pre-computed patterns; low overhead [S1][S2].
+**Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of shift patterns; specific to SIMD architectures [S1][S2].
+**When to use:** When decompression speed is critical and the hardware supports SIMD operations with variable bit-width extraction [S1][S2].
+**When not to use:** On hardware without SIMD support or where pre-computation is not feasible [S1][S2].
 ## Background
-Originated with Smelyanskiy et al. [SHC* 09] as a differences-based compression scheme for GPU-based volume rendering [S1][S2]. Mensmann et al. [MRH10] proposed a hybrid variant incorporating variable-length coding and delta encoding [S1]. It is used in compressed GPU-based direct volume rendering to reduce memory bandwidth requirements [S1][S2].
-
+Differences-based Compression was employed by Smelyanskiy et al. [SHC* 09] in the context of compressed GPU-based direct volume rendering [S1][S2]. It is part of the broader family of predictive and differential coding methods for volume data [S1][S2].
